{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Chicago Cubs were a team in transition, caught between the remnants of a championship core and an emerging group of young players who weren't quite ready to carry the load. They stumbled out of the gate in April and May, going a combined 20-29 over those two months as their bullpen repeatedly failed them in high-leverage moments. Rowan Wick, Scott Effross, and Daniel Norris were torched in back-to-back-to-back disasters in late May, and a costly error by Wick on July 3rd cost them a pivotal game against Boston. The early chapters of the season read like a series of gut punches, with opposing hitters feasting on a thin pitching staff.\n\nJune brought little relief, as the Cubs posted a miserable 10-17 record and watched their playoff hopes shrink. Yet there were scattered flickers of life — Alfonso Rivas delivered a clutch walk-off single against Pittsburgh on June 23rd, and the Fourth of July weekend offered a rollercoaster, with Seiya Suzuki thrilling the Wrigley crowd with a rare inside-the-park home run before the bullpen collapsed again. The Cubs were a team of moments, not a team of momentum.\n\nAugust told a more complicated story. The record was an even 15-15, but it masked genuine drama. The month produced the season's most emotionally dense stretch — Willson Contreras, playing out what felt like a farewell tour, hit a go-ahead home run on August 5th. Then came an extraordinary August 20th against Milwaukee, where the Cubs erased a Willy Adames homer with a furious rally sparked by Patrick Wisdom, Nick Madrigal, and Contreras himself. For one night, the old magic was back.\n\nSeptember and October offered a surprising coda. The Cubs went 18-13 to close the year, their best stretch of the season. Young players like Christopher Morel showed flashes of a brighter future, while veterans dug in for one last push. It wasn't enough to reach the postseason — the 74-88 record told the truth — but the late-season surge at least gave fans something to hold onto as another rebuild quietly began.",
